  "conclusion": "Chromatic pixels are sparse: only 8/57 validation and 7/55 development query interiors contain any under this threshold, and ring color appears in 16/57 and 11/55 contexts. After evaluator query/context exclusions, ring hue is no more aligned with validation positives than reviewed negatives (median cosine 0.029 vs 0.141; positive similarity is higher in 8/16 colored-ring cases). The real development comparison is mixed and small (0.203 vs 0.042; higher positive similarity in 6/11 cases). q610 is a clear counterexample: its query and scored positive sample are achromatic; cyan from the pad also occurs in reviewed negatives. The evidence does not support a hard color gate or a claim of general color benefit. If explored, append color as a soft feature with an explicit absent-color state and evaluate only with fit-only training plus held-out development; this remains a hypothesis, not an expected gain.",
  "limitations": [
    "No per-pixel model probabilities were available to analyze in this extension.",
    "Hue histograms omit spatial arrangement and can agree because of a few colored pixels; they do not establish better IoU or precision.",
    "Validation is public and diagnostic, not independent generalization evidence.",
    "The earlier crop contact sheet shows original RGB crops for q610 and its highest-FP same-document sibling; positive and reviewed-negative crops are illustrative, while cohort summaries sample up to 5,000 pixels per class."
  ]
